   "body": "> Fixes #329946 (partial — see \"Known limitations\" below)\n> \n> ## Summary\n> \n> When the terminal panel is resized, the shell prompt can be duplicated / new prompt lines created on every resize instead of the buffer reflowing cleanly. Two causes in the terminal resize path:\n> \n> 1. **Redundant PTY resizes on every layout event.** `TerminalResizeDebouncer.resize()` fired a resize even when the grid dimensions did not change (e.g. while dragging the panel), sending a PTY resize (SIGWINCH) on every layout event. Shells repaint the prompt on SIGWINCH, so this produced a storm of prompt redraws while dragging.\n> \n> 2. **Horizontal resizes debounced too briefly.** The debounced horizontal resize fired after 100ms, but shells such as zsh need longer than that to fully process a prompt redraw after a resize. When resizes arrived back-to-back, the shell computed its prompt redraw from a stale terminal width, which misplaces the erase/rewrite and leaves duplicated prompt fragments in the buffer.\n> \n> ## Changes\n> \n> `src/vs/workbench/contrib/terminal/browser/terminalResizeDebouncer.ts`:\n> - Skip the resize entirely (and cancel any pending debounced resize) when the requested `cols`/`rows` equal the current grid — no more redundant PTY resizes / SIGWINCH storms while dragging.\n> - Only fire the immediate Y-resize when `rows` actually changed, and only schedule the debounced X-resize when `cols` actually changed.\n> - Increase `DebounceResizeXDelay` from 100ms to 300ms so the shell's prompt redraw is fully processed between consecutive resizes.\n> \n> ## Verification\n> \n> - Reproduced the duplication headlessly with a real zsh 5.9 and bash shell in a pty driving the bundled xterm.js: with the 100ms debounce the buffer ended with 3 prompt copies after a shrink/grow/shrink cycle; with 300ms exactly 1 copy.\n> - Verified in a running Code OSS instance (launched with playwright over CDP): resizing the bottom-panel terminal (long prompt, shrink/grow cycles, continuous drags) reflows cleanly with no duplication.\n> \n> ## Known limitations (honest assessment)\n> \n> This fixes the common cases (panel resizing at moderate widths, continuous drags). It does **not** fix every duplication scenario:\n> \n> - **Extreme-narrow terminals with colored prompts** (e.g. terminal docked in the side panel and shrunk to its minimum width, ~15-20 columns, with a colored `PS1`/`PROMPT`): the prompt can still duplicate. Root cause: xterm.js deliberately does not reflow the cursor line on non-Windows (`reflowCursorLine=false`), so the prompt is a single buffer row rendered wrapped across several visual rows. The shell's redraw (readline/zsh) operates on visual rows, so its erase/rewrite misses rows and fragments accumulate. Enabling `reflowCursorLine` makes this worse due to https://github.com/xtermjs/xterm.js/issues/5295 (cursor mispositioned on reflow of cursor line), which the xterm.js maintainers have declined to fix. Upstream VS Code has no fix for this either; it would require a change in xterm.js or readline.\n> \n> ---\n> \n> **Restored PR:** This replaces #330040, which was closed automatically when my fork was deleted from GitHub. GitHub cannot reopen a PR whose head repository was deleted, so the branch was rebuilt from the original commit (same message and authorship) on top of the current `main`. Please review this PR in place of #330040.\n> \n\n",
